export const DE_B2_S02_ENGLISH: Record<string, string[]> = {
  "de-hundertachtzig-meter-taft": [
    `In the summer of 1979 Gudrun Mehnert had been working in the fabric department of the department store in Saalfeld for eleven years, and she believed she knew her customers inside out. There were the women sewing for their daughter's Jugendweihe, the men reluctantly buying curtains, and the hoarders who took whatever happened to be there, because in the GDR nobody knew when there would next be anything to buy.`,
    `The man who came up to her counter one Tuesday in July fitted none of these groups. He was polite, in his mid-thirties, and he wanted taffeta. Not three metres, not ten, but everything they had. When Gudrun asked him what he needed so much fabric for, he explained without hesitating that his camping club wanted to sew tents. Gudrun measured it out: a hundred and eighty metres, red and blue stripes. He paid cash and carried the bolts out himself to a Trabant whose springs groaned alarmingly.`,
    `Two weeks later a woman asked for umbrella fabric, also in large quantities, supposedly for a sailing club. Gudrun might have forgotten about it if her colleague Renate hadn't raised her eyebrows. "Tents and sails," Renate whispered. "And here of all places, where there isn't a lake or a campsite for miles around. You should report that, Gudrun." Everyone knew what "report" meant, and to whom.`,
    `Gudrun reported nothing. She couldn't have said exactly why herself. Perhaps because of her brother, who in August 1961, at nineteen, had climbed over a wall in Berlin that back then wasn't yet a proper wall, and from whom she had received only Christmas cards ever since. Perhaps simply because the man had said "thank you" so warmly, as if he were thanking her for something much bigger than fabric.`,
    `On the morning of 16 September she listened secretly to Western radio and heard that two families from Thuringia had flown across the border during the night in a home-made hot-air balloon and landed near Naila in Upper Franconia, four adults and four children. The envelope, the announcer said, was made of countless strips of fabric sewn together. Gudrun switched off the radio and sat down on the kitchen chair. Her coffee went cold.`,
    `The men from the Stasi came as early as Wednesday. They asked every saleswoman in the district whether she had noticed any large purchases of fabric in recent months. Gudrun answered that she sold fabric to dozens of people every day and couldn't possibly remember them all. Renate, who was standing next to her, said she knew nothing either. After that the two women never spoke about the matter again, not even after 1989, when suddenly you were allowed to talk about everything.`,
    `Forty years later, in the autumn of 2019, Gudrun travelled to Regensburg with her granddaughter. The balloon hangs in a museum there today, or at least a large part of it: an enormous patchwork of fabrics in every possible colour and pattern. Her granddaughter read the information panel aloud. Gudrun wasn't listening. She was searching. After a quarter of an hour she found what she was looking for: red and blue stripes, sewn together a little crookedly, with a selvedge she had cut herself with her big scissors back then.`,
    `"Grandma, why are you crying?" her granddaughter asked. "I'm not crying," said Gudrun, and blew her nose. "A hundred and eighty metres. And he paid cash."`,
  ],
  "de-die-letzte-deutschlehrerin": [
    `In 2041 Renate Kowalski was teaching the last German course in the Ruhr area at the adult education centre in Bochum, and she knew it. Ever since the little translators had existed, which you put in your ear like earplugs and which converted every spoken word into any language you liked in real time, hardly anyone needed a foreign language any more. The devices were cheap, reliable and had become as much a matter of course as mobile phones once were. The course "German B2, evenings" had a single participant: Oksana Melnyk, an engineer from Kharkiv, who had been in Bochum for four years.`,
    `In the first lesson Renate had asked her why she was learning German anyway. Oksana had shrugged. The device translated everything, she said, but only what was actually said. When her boss said "Das ist ja interessant", she heard the translation and still didn't know whether he was delighted or thought she was mad. "I want to understand the pauses," she had said. Renate hadn't replied, but she had written the sentence down.`,
    `The course went differently from every course Renate had taught in thirty years. There were no textbooks any more, because no publisher printed them any more. So they read newspapers, recipes, medicine leaflets and old love letters from Renate's drawer. They practised modal particles until Oksana not only understood the difference between "Komm mal her" and "Komm doch mal her" but could hear it too. In February the management of the adult education centre announced that the course would be discontinued in the summer. One participant simply wasn't a course.`,
    `On 14 March at 9.12 an unusually violent solar storm reached the Earth. The power grids held, but the servers through which all the translators ran collapsed for nineteen hours. All of a sudden millions of people stood in front of each other and didn't understand a word. At Bochum's main station a conductor made announcements that only half the passengers understood. In the hospitals people searched desperately for anyone who had a command of more than one language.`,
    `Oksana's company, a manufacturer of wind turbines, had a video conference that morning with a big customer from Bavaria. The contract had been prepared for months; the signature was meant to be a mere formality. Then the translation went down, and in the conference room sat eleven engineers from seven countries, smiling helplessly at the screen. Until Oksana cleared her throat and said in German that, if it was all right, she would take over the negotiations.`,
    `She led them for three hours. When the customer said they would "still have to talk about the price again", she understood that he wasn't dissatisfied but wanted to negotiate. When at the end he said "Passt scho", she understood that in Bavaria this was about the highest praise there is. By the evening the contract was signed, Oksana's boss had promised her a promotion, and the servers were running again.`,
    `When Renate came into the adult education centre's office a week later to collect her papers, there was a pile of registrations on the table: twenty-seven for "German B2, evenings". The head of the centre looked at her sheepishly. Could Renate perhaps carry on with the course after all? They had obviously underestimated something there. Renate took the pile, leafed through it slowly and let herself be asked for what she felt was an appropriate length of time.`,
    `In the first lesson of the new course she wrote a single sentence on the board: "I want to understand the pauses." In the back row sat Oksana, who no longer had to come and had come anyway, and smiled.`,
  ],
  "de-die-zeugin-im-dritten-stock": [
    `Gertrud Hambach was eighty-four, had a cataract in her left eye and a flat on the third floor of an Art Nouveau building in Wiesbaden's Rheingau quarter, from which you could see half the street. She didn't regard spending most of the day at the window as curiosity, but as a kind of voluntary service. After all, somebody had to keep an eye on things.`,
    `On the Thursday before Whitsun, shortly after eleven at night, she watched two men carry a long object wrapped in a carpet out of the house opposite and heave it with difficulty into a white van. The carpet seemed to be heavy. One of the men was Herr Lindner from the second floor, a polite insurance broker who always said hello. The other she had never seen. Frau Lindner, Gertrud knew, had been away on a business trip in Hamburg since Monday. At least that was what Herr Lindner had claimed.`,
    `Gertrud called the police. The young officer on the phone listened patiently, asked her age twice and promised to send a patrol round. The patrol came the next morning. Herr Lindner opened the door in his dressing gown, looked surprised but not at all worried, and explained that he had taken an old carpet to the bulky waste collection. His wife was in Hamburg; they were welcome to call her. The officers didn't call. On the way out one of them cast a long look up at Gertrud's window, which she understood perfectly well.`,
    `Gertrud wasn't offended, at least not for long. She was just sure that something wasn't right. Over the following days she noted everything in a little notebook: that Herr Lindner no longer turned on the light in his living room in the evenings, that he left the house twice with big cardboard boxes, that the white van came back on Sunday. Her granddaughter, who visited her every Wednesday, said Grandma watched too many crime shows. Gertrud retorted that she didn't watch any crime shows at all; after all, she had a window.`,
    `On the Tuesday after Whitsun a taxi stopped outside the house opposite. A woman with a wheeled suitcase got out, unlocked the front door and came out again about ten minutes later, without the suitcase but with a sheet of paper in her hand. She sat down on the steps in front of the entrance and didn't move any more. It was Frau Lindner. So she was alive. Gertrud was relieved and, as she later admitted to herself, just a tiny bit disappointed.`,
    `Then she looked more closely. The woman on the steps was crying. Gertrud put on her cardigan, took the lift, which she otherwise never did, and crossed the street. Without a word Frau Lindner held out the note to her. It was a letter from her husband, short and matter-of-fact. He had fallen in love with someone else, a year ago already, and hadn't been able to tell her to her face. He had taken his furniture, as well as the Persian carpet from the living room. He was leaving her the rest.`,
    `"All week," said Frau Lindner, "he told me on the phone every evening how much he missed me." Gertrud sat down next to her on the cold steps, which her knees held against her for days afterwards. She told her about the carpet, the police and the little notebook. Frau Lindner listened, and at some point, in spite of everything, she had to laugh. "You thought he had murdered me?" "Honestly, yes," said Gertrud. "I'd never have thought he was such a coward."`,
    `Since then Frau Lindner, who now goes by her maiden name Kessler again, has visited the old lady every Thursday evening. They drink tea at the window on the third floor and look down at the street. Gertrud has kept the notebook. You never know, she says, what you might need it for one day.`,
  ],
  "de-das-wasser-von-altona": [
    `Hamburg, 20 August 1892. Dear Anna, forgive me for not writing for so long. For two weeks it has been unbearably hot in Hamburg, the canals stink, and at the hospital in Eppendorf we hardly get any sleep. Every day they bring in new patients with vomiting and diarrhoea, above all from the harbour and the narrow back-alley quarters. The doctors say it is the usual summer sickness, and the Senate has announced in the newspapers that there is no cause for concern. I wish I could believe it. Those who are admitted to us are often dead by the next morning.`,
    `Hamburg, 25 August. Dear Anna, now it is official: it is cholera. Yesterday Professor Koch came from Berlin, a small, serious man with glasses, and found the germs under the microscope at once. People say the Senate kept the disease quiet for days so that the ships would go on docking in the harbour and the merchants would not lose money. Whether that is true I don't know. I only know that patients are now lying in our corridors, because the beds stopped being enough long ago.`,
    `Hamburg, 30 August. Dear Anna, I have noticed something, and I must tell someone. Almost all our patients come from Hamburg, hardly any from Altona, although Altona is right next door and the people there are just as poor. A policeman we admitted with fever told me that in a street the border runs through, whole families had fallen ill on the Hamburg side, but nobody on the Altona side. The only difference I can think of is the water. Altona filters its Elbe water through sand before it goes into the pipes; Hamburg pumps it unfiltered out of the river. I told our ward doctor. He said I should leave such questions to the learned men.`,
    `Hamburg, 3 September. Dear Anna, I have not left it to the learned men. Since Monday I have been boiling every drop of drinking water for our ward at night on the stove in the nurses' room; I pay for the coal myself. Head Nurse Hedwig raged: it had not been ordered, it was a waste, and a nurse was there to care for the sick, not to do research. Even so, I give the relatives who visit our patients slips of paper saying that at home they should drink only boiled water. Most of them can't read. So I tell them, again and again, until they can repeat it.`,
    `Hamburg, 12 September. Dear Anna, now there are Senate posters on every street corner: tap water may only be drunk boiled. The city even sends carts with boiled water through the neighbourhoods. On our ward not a single nurse or orderly has been infected for three weeks. Head Nurse Hedwig now supervises the boiling personally, with a strictness as if it had been her own idea. I let her have her pleasure. Little Paul from my landlady's house, who came to us two weeks ago with severe cramps, is also out of the woods. Yesterday he laughed again for the first time.`,
    `Hamburg, 2 November. Dear Anna, the epidemic is over. More than eight thousand people died, in only ten weeks. The newspapers now write that the catastrophe could have been prevented if the city had built a filtration plant in time; the plans for it had apparently been lying in some drawer for years. Now, at last, it is being built. I am tired, Anna, but not unhappy. Recently the ward doctor asked me how I had hit upon the water back then. I answered that I had only listened when a policeman with a fever told me about his street. For a long time he said nothing. Then he asked me to teach the young nurses the rules of hygiene this winter. Your sister Marie`,
  ],
  "de-der-kollege-den-es-nicht-gab": [
    `Nobody knew exactly any more who had invented Herr Krause. In the complaints department of a haulage firm in Mülheim an der Ruhr, everyone claimed it had been someone else. The only certain thing was that it had begun about four years ago, on a Friday afternoon, when an angry customer called because his delivery had disappeared, and Sabine Özdemir said into the phone: "That was handled by our colleague Herr Krause, who unfortunately isn't at his desk right now."`,
    `From then on Krause was indispensable. He was to blame for lost documents, missed deadlines and the broken coffee machine on the fourth floor. He was always in a meeting, at the dentist or on holiday. Over time he acquired a personality: he rode a racing bike, was allergic to nuts and had a difficult relationship with his mother-in-law in Oberhausen. Colleagues from other departments sent him birthday greetings. Martin Weber from IT, who joined in the game with enthusiasm, had even set up an email account for him, from which everyone took turns replying.`,
    `The thing only got out of control when the firm got a new head of HR in the spring. Dr. Annette Wirtz came from Munich, had a reputation for being thorough and had resolved to get to know every employee personally. Going through the internal communications, she came across a name that had appeared in over three hundred emails in the past twelve months, mostly in connection with problems that had then been miraculously solved. She was impressed. A man who handled so many crises deserved a promotion.`,
    `The invitation came by email to the account Martin had set up: Herr Krause should report to her on Monday at ten o'clock; it was about a position as team leader. On Friday the mood in the complaints department was that of a crisis committee. They considered everything. They could call Krause in sick. They could have him resign. They could also, someone suggested, let him die, which Sabine found tasteless and Martin found technically too complicated.`,
    `In the end it was Sabine who decided to sort the matter out herself. On Monday at ten she knocked on the head of HR's door, sat down, took a deep breath and explained everything: the phone call four years ago, the racing bike, the mother-in-law, the three hundred emails. She expected a formal warning, if not something worse. It was important to her, however, to stress, she added, that it had never been about cheating customers. The problems had in fact been solved, just not by Herr Krause.`,
    `Dr. Wirtz listened in silence. Then she wanted to know who had solved the problems. Sabine hesitated. Mostly herself, she finally admitted, sometimes Martin, occasionally all of them together. It had simply been more pleasant to pin a mistake on a man who didn't exist than to spend ages asking who had really made it. That way nobody had had to be afraid, and everyone had helped.`,
    `The head of HR leaned back and was silent for so long that Sabine began to fear she would have to pack her things any minute. "Do you know what I had at my last company?" Dr. Wirtz said at last. "Thirty real employees who all blamed each other. You, on the other hand, have a department that needs an invented scapegoat to be able to talk openly about mistakes. Honestly, I don't know which is worse."`,
    `Sabine Özdemir got the job as team leader. Herr Krause was not dismissed. His email account still exists today, and whenever something goes wrong in the complaints department, he is still to blame. In June he even received a birthday card from the head of HR.`,
  ],
};

